# ViroVision app — environment variables (see ADR 0002).
# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in from your Supabase project settings.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* vars are inlined into the JS bundle and available at runtime via process.env.

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=YOUR-ANON-KEY

# --- Supermarket mode: cloud vision models (ADR 0006 + ADR 0008) ---
#
# ⚠️ EXPO_PUBLIC_* variables are INLINED into the JS bundle: they are not environment variables the
#    binary reads at startup, they are constants compiled inside the .ipa/.apk. A `strings` over the
#    bundle returns them, and since there is a public TestFlight link anyone can install it.
#    That is why the keys' destination is the ADR 0008 PROXY, and this direct path stays as the
#    development one, against a local .env.
#
# 🔑 RULE while the proxy is not deployed (ADR 0008):
#
#      A FREE key with no card may go into the bundle. A PAID key may not.
#
#    The worst case of a stolen free key is a burnt quota that replenishes itself; the worst case of
#    a paid one is the project's card. So Gemini and Groq can be loaded as repo secrets and travel in
#    the TestFlight/Play builds; OpenAI and Anthropic live ONLY here, in your .env, until the proxy
#    exists.
#
#    With the proxy on the rule stops mattering: no key travels at all.
#
#    Careful: if the build goes out with no key for any provider IN THE REGISTRY and with no proxy,
#    supermarket mode is dead. `npm run keys` detects it and fails the job before the build — it
#    really happened on 2026-09-02.
#
# Each model in Home's selector appears ONLY if its provider has a key in this build. With none,
# supermarket mode says it is not configured; bus mode (local OCR) keeps working — it is the path
# ADR 0001 requires without internet.

# --- IN THE SELECTOR ---

# OpenAI — the DEFAULT. Model gpt-5.6-luna: 1668 ms median measured on 2026-09-02, generous quota,
# ~USD 0.0003 per reading. PAID: it requires credit at platform.openai.com.
# ⚠️ Set a SPENDING CAP in the console before generating the key: it is the only safety net that does
#    not depend on our code being right.
# ⚠️ Do NOT load it as a repo secret until the proxy is deployed (see the rule above).
EXPO_PUBLIC_OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Groq — the fastest: 846 ms median. FREE, no card (console.groq.com). Model qwen/qwen3.8-27b on
# their LPUs — the "different hardware" point of comparison in the experiment.
# Its quota limits by TOKENS per minute (~4 readings), which is why it is not the default.
# It can go as a repo secret: it is free.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GROQ_API_KEY=

# --- RETIRED FROM THE SELECTOR (their providers are still implemented; see RETIRED_PROFILES) ---
# Loading these keys does NOT bring them back to the selector: their profile has to be moved into
# MODEL_PROFILES.

# Gemini — it was the default until 2026-09-02. It left over the measurement: a 2820-32 586 ms range.
# Free without a card (aistudio.google.com), and it keeps the best quota of all (20/min).
EXPO_PUBLIC_GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Anthropic — never verified against its API: it requires credit at console.anthropic.com (a Claude
# subscription does NOT enable the API).
EXPO_PUBLIC_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- The key proxy (ADR 0008) ---
#
# With this set, the app needs NONE of the keys above: the server holds them, and every model shows
# up in the selector all the same. It is what makes a build distributable.
# Without it, the app calls the provider directly with the key from .env — the development path.
#
# Deployment and secrets: docs/supabase.md.
EXPO_PUBLIC_VISION_PROXY_URL=

# --- Audio file of the reading (OFF by default) ---
#
# Set 1 so that, on top of the voice announcement, every reading leaves an .mp3 on disk. It is the
# input for the device's speaker (see the diagram in docs/architecture/README.md); `expo-speech` uses
# the system engine and does not export to a file, so the .mp3 comes from a cloud TTS and COSTS one
# call per reading.
#
# It is off because nothing consumes it today: the hardware does not exist, and when it does it may
# be better for the Raspberry Pi to do its own TTS and only receive the JSON (ADR 0003, unwritten).
# Turn it on when you have something to test it with. It needs an OpenAI key or the proxy.
EXPO_PUBLIC_AUDIO_FILE_ENABLED=

# --- Simulated device (DEVELOPMENT / DEMO ONLY) ---
# Set 1 so "Buscar dispositivo" returns a fake device with battery and firmware, so the
# connected-device screen can be seen and demoed without hardware. The UI labels it as simulated:
# this does NOT pretend BLE works. CAREFUL: with 1 the real BLE client stays off even if the build
# has it; to measure against the device (ADR 0003) it has to be empty or 0.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SIMULATE_DEVICE=

# --- Telemetry (`events` table in Supabase) ---
#
# URL of the `telemetry` function, which receives batches of events and inserts them with the service
# role:
#   https://<project>.supabase.co/functions/v1/telemetry
#
# It is a URL, not a secret: the function requires no authentication (`verify_jwt = false`, same as
# the vision proxy) because the app has no login and an anon key in the bundle would not be a defence.
#
# It does NOT need to be set if EXPO_PUBLIC_VISION_PROXY_URL already is: telemetry derives its URL
# from that one (…/functions/v1/vision → …/functions/v1/telemetry), because both functions live in
# the same project. This is for pointing at another project without touching the proxy, and nothing
# else.
#
# With BOTH empty, telemetry stays off entirely: it does not enqueue, retry or weigh anything. Be
# careful about leaving it like that in a build going to TestFlight: since 2026-09-08 the technical
# information is NOT on the screens and this table is the only diagnosis left.
EXPO_PUBLIC_TELEMETRY_URL=
